# OSI & TCP/IP Models

## 1. Introduction

The **OSI Model** and **TCP/IP Model** are networking reference models used to understand how data travels from one device to another over a network.

They divide network communication into different layers. Each layer has a specific responsibility and works with the layers above and below it.

The two important models are:

* **OSI Model** — 7 layers
* **TCP/IP Model** — 4 layers

---

# 2. OSI Model

OSI stands for **Open Systems Interconnection**.

It was developed by the **International Organization for Standardization (ISO)** to provide a standard framework for understanding network communication.

The OSI model has **7 layers**:

| Layer | Name         | Main Responsibility                  | Data Unit        |
| ----- | ------------ | ------------------------------------ | ---------------- |
| 7     | Application  | Network services for applications    | Data             |
| 6     | Presentation | Translation, encryption, compression | Data             |
| 5     | Session      | Establish/manage/terminate sessions  | Data             |
| 4     | Transport    | End-to-end delivery                  | Segment/Datagram |
| 3     | Network      | Logical addressing and routing       | Packet           |
| 2     | Data Link    | MAC addressing and frames            | Frame            |
| 1     | Physical     | Transmission of bits                 | Bits             |

### Easy way to remember

From Layer 7 → Layer 1:

**A P S T N D P**

> All People Seem To Need Data Processing

From Layer 1 → Layer 7:

**P D N T S P A**

> Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away

---

# 3. Layer 7 — Application Layer

## Definition

The Application Layer is the **top layer of the OSI model**.

It provides network services directly to applications used by users.

It does not mean applications themselves are Layer 7. Instead, it provides network-related services that applications use.

## Functions

* Provides network services to applications
* Allows applications to communicate over the network
* Supports services such as web browsing, email and file transfer
* Identifies available communication services

## Common Protocols

* HTTP
* HTTPS
* FTP
* SMTP
* DNS
* DHCP
* SSH
* Telnet
* SNMP

## Examples

When you open:

`https://example.com`

Your browser uses application-layer protocols such as **HTTPS** to communicate with the web server.

## Troubleshooting

If a website is not opening:

* Check whether the application is working
* Check DNS resolution
* Check whether the required port is accessible
* Check HTTP/HTTPS connectivity

---

# 4. Layer 6 — Presentation Layer

## Definition

The Presentation Layer is responsible for how data is **formatted, translated, encrypted and compressed**.

It ensures that data sent by one system can be understood by another system.

## Functions

### 1. Data Translation

Converts data between different formats.

Example:

ASCII ↔ EBCDIC

### 2. Encryption/Decryption

Provides data encryption and decryption.

Example:

TLS is commonly associated with the security of application communication, although modern protocol stacks don't always map neatly to one OSI layer.

### 3. Compression/Decompression

Reduces the size of data before transmission.

## Examples

* Data encoding
* Encryption
* Compression
* Character conversion

## Troubleshooting

Possible issues include:

* Encryption/certificate problems
* Incorrect data format
* Compression/decompression problems

---

# 5. Layer 5 — Session Layer

## Definition

The Session Layer establishes, manages and terminates communication sessions between applications.

## Functions

* Establishes sessions
* Maintains sessions
* Terminates sessions
* Provides synchronization
* Helps recover communication after interruptions

## Example

Suppose two systems establish a communication session.

The Session Layer conceptually manages:

```text
Session Start
     ↓
Data Exchange
     ↓
Session Management
     ↓
Session End
```

## Troubleshooting

Possible symptoms:

* Session disconnects
* Connection repeatedly drops
* Application session cannot be maintained

