# 11. Encapsulation

## Definition

Encapsulation is the process of adding control information such as headers and trailers to data as it moves **down the OSI layers** before transmission.

Example:

```text
Application
    ↓
Data

Transport
    ↓
Segment

Network
    ↓
Packet

Data Link
    ↓
Frame

Physical
    ↓
Bits
```

## Detailed Flow

```text
Application Data
       ↓
+ Transport Header
       ↓
Segment
       ↓
+ IP Header
       ↓
Packet
       ↓
+ Ethernet Header/Trailer
       ↓
Frame
       ↓
Bits
```

The exact headers depend on the protocols being used.

---

# 12. Decapsulation

Decapsulation is the reverse process.

When the receiving device receives the bits, the data moves **up through the layers**.

```text
Bits
  ↓
Frame
  ↓
Packet
  ↓
Segment
  ↓
Data
```

Each layer processes and removes the information relevant to it before passing the remaining data upward.

---

# 13. Encapsulation vs Decapsulation

| Encapsulation                          | Decapsulation                          |
| -------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Happens at sender                      | Happens at receiver                    |
| Data moves downward                    | Data moves upward                      |
| Headers/trailers are added             | Headers/trailers are processed/removed |
| Data → Segment → Packet → Frame → Bits | Bits → Frame → Packet → Segment → Data |

---

# 14. TCP/IP Model

The TCP/IP model is the practical networking model used by the Internet and modern networks.

It traditionally has **4 layers**:

| TCP/IP Layer   | Main Responsibility                      |
| -------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Application    | Application services                     |
| Transport      | End-to-end communication                 |
| Internet       | IP addressing and routing                |
| Network Access | Local delivery and physical transmission |

---

# 15. TCP/IP Application Layer

This layer combines the functions of the OSI:

* Application
* Presentation
* Session

## Protocols

* HTTP
* HTTPS
* DNS
* DHCP
* FTP
* SMTP
* SSH
* SNMP

---

# 16. TCP/IP Transport Layer

Responsible for end-to-end communication.

Main protocols:

* TCP
* UDP

Responsibilities:

* Segmentation
* Port numbers
* Reliability
* Flow control
* Error control

---

# 17. TCP/IP Internet Layer

Responsible for logical addressing and routing.

Protocols:

* IPv4
* IPv6
* ICMP

Main device:

* Router

Data unit:

**Packet**

---

# 18. TCP/IP Network Access Layer

Responsible for local network communication and physical transmission.

It combines the functions of:

* OSI Data Link Layer
* OSI Physical Layer

Examples:

* Ethernet
* Wi-Fi
* MAC addressing
* Network interface
* Physical media

---

# 19. OSI vs TCP/IP

| OSI                                      | TCP/IP                                    |
| ---------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| 7 layers                                 | 4 layers traditionally                    |
| Reference model                          | Practical protocol suite/model            |
| Developed by ISO                         | Developed from ARPA/DARPA Internet work   |
| More detailed separation                 | More consolidated layers                  |
| Session and Presentation are separate    | Included in Application                   |
| Physical and Data Link are separate      | Combined as Network Access                |
| Mainly used for learning/troubleshooting | Used extensively in real-world networking |

### Mapping

```text
OSI                         TCP/IP

Application       ┐
Presentation      ├──────→  Application
Session           ┘

Transport         ───────→  Transport

Network           ───────→  Internet

Data Link         ┐
Physical          ┘──────→  Network Access
```

---

# 20. OSI Model in Real-Life Web Browsing

Suppose you open:

```text
https://www.example.com
```

A simplified flow is:

### Layer 7 — Application

Browser uses HTTPS and DNS-related services.

### Layer 6 — Presentation

Data may involve encoding and encryption-related processing.

### Layer 5 — Session

Communication sessions are managed.

### Layer 4 — Transport

TCP provides reliable transport where applicable, using port 443 for HTTPS.

### Layer 3 — Network

IP addressing and routing determine where packets should go.

### Layer 2 — Data Link

Ethernet/Wi-Fi uses frames and MAC addresses for local delivery.

### Layer 1 — Physical

Bits travel through:

* Ethernet cable
* Fiber
* Wi-Fi radio

